SET explanation = $res28$Communication planning becomes increasingly important in a virtual team environment. Additional time may be needed to set clear expectations, facilitate communications, develop protocols for resolving conflict, include people in decision making, understand cultural differences, and share credit for success. When the project team is located in the same physical space, communication is much easier and more effective. Face-to-face communication is richer and is the most effective form of communication. Tone and non-verbal communication may be lost in a virtual environment. Additionally, virtual teams lack the intangible benefits of colocated teams who are able to bond more easily and become more cohesive. Therefore, communication planning will require special consideration while working with a virtual team. Details for Each Option: A Project expenses Incorrect. One of the advantages of the team members working from their home offices as a virtual team is the expense savings associated with offices and physical equipment needed for employees if they were working in the company’s offices. Therefore, project expenses are not a special consideration for a virtual team, making this choice an incorrect response. B Communication planning Correct. Communication planning becomes increasingly important in a virtual team environment. Additional time may be needed to set clear expectations, facilitate communications, develop protocols for resolving conflict, include people in decision making, understand cultural differences, and share credit for success. Therefore, communication planning should be specifically considered while the work is performed by a virtual team. C Pre-assigned team members Incorrect. When physical or team resources for a project are determined in advance, they are considered pre-assigned. Whether or not the project team members were pre-assigned has no bearing on the team operating in a virtual environment. Therefore, this answer can be eliminated. D Team members with disabilities or mobility issues Incorrect. The question states the team members will be allowed to work from their home offices. Therefore, individuals with disabilities or mobility issues will be able to work from home without a need to commute to their local office. Thus, no special considerations should be taken into account with the use of a virtual team when some of the team members have disabilities or mobility issues.$res28$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $res28$RESOURCE-33$res28$;

-- Q34: RESOURCE-34 explanation (970 -> 2076 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $res28$Ken Blanchard and Paul Hershey developed a situational leadership styles model mapping it to Tuckman's model for the stages of team formation: forming, storming, norming, performing, and adjourning. According to the situational leadership model, teams in the forming stage are characterized by low competence and high commitment and therefore need little support but a lot of direction from their leaders. In storming, the teams are characterized by some competence and low commitment and therefore need a lot of coaching, support, and direction from their leaders. The norming stage requires a lot of support but less direction. Finally, performing teams need nothing but mostly delegating. In this question, the most logical conclusion that the agile practitioner could have come to was that the team most likely got "stuck" in the storming stage, which according to the situational leadership model, requires a lot of direction from their leader/manager. Details for Each Option: A The team is still in the storming stage, which requires a lot of direction. Correct. Teams in storming stage of their formation need a lot of support and direction from their managers. B The team is already in the norming stage, which requires a lot of direction. Incorrect. Teams in norming stage of their formation needs a lot of support, but little direction from their managers. C The project has a very tight schedule and must be managed by directives. Incorrect. While using a directive approach may be appropriate in some extreme situations, typically the project schedule is not supposed to affect leadership style. Being highly directive does not necessarily mean the project will be delivered on schedule. D Theory is one thing, and real life is a totally different story. Incorrect. It is hard to argue with this statement. However, as far as Agile is concerned, leadership style during different team formation stages plays a significant role in the team's and project's success. Therefore, applying battle-tested agile theories on real life agile projects is highly recommended.$res28$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $res28$RESOURCE-34$res28$;

-- Q35: RESOURCE-35 explanation (876 -> 2547 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $res28$The question describes a project that is transitioning from a predictive phase to an agile phase, specifically Scrumban. When using a predictive model, tasks are typically assigned to specific individuals and then recorded in the project schedule. When an agile framework is employed, project tasks (user stories in this case) are assigned at the team level rather than the individual level, and the project team then collaborates to determine how the tasks will be completed and by whom. A team charter that was developed for a predictive phase is not likely to contain agreements for how the work will be assigned. Therefore, the project manager should revisit the team's social contract (team charter) before the start of an agile phase and have the team align on how they will collaborate to complete the work, rather than making the assignments for the team. Details for Each Option: A Facilitated an update to the team charter for the agile phase of the project Correct. When using a Kanban board, the team will need to collaborate to complete the required work. How the team will collaborate to determine work assignments should be reflected in the team's social contract (team charter). B Avoided different project management approaches for different phases Incorrect. In some cases, projects may benefit by combining project management frameworks. For example, a project with a development phase that is characterized by unclear requirements and high rates of change might benefit from an agile approach within an otherwise predictive model. When a traditional waterfall model is combined with agile methodologies, the project becomes a hybrid. C Used eXtreme Programming framework instead of the Scrumban method Incorrect. eXtreme Programming, similar to Scrumban, is one of the agile frameworks. When any of the agile frameworks are used, tasks are typically pulled by the team members rather than assigned by a manager. The essence of the question reflects an issue with 'how' the work is managed rather than a project management approach used. D Nothing could have been done differently since conflict is unavoidable. Incorrect. While it is reasonable to assume that some level of conflict is likely unavoidable on any project, stating that nothing could have been done differently is far going. Project managers should apply their leadership, managerial, and interpersonal skills to try to resolve conflicts. The situation described in the scenario does not look unresolvable. One of the answer choices provides a plausible solution.$res28$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $res28$RESOURCE-35$res28$;

-- Q37: RESOURCE-37 explanation (1000 -> 3218 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $res28$The scenario describes a situation where the development team wants to try pair programming as a strategy to improve productivity. Even though you, as the project manager, are skeptical that the approach will be effective, you should still support the project team with their efforts to improve productivity. After all, the purpose of a sprint retrospective is for the project team to inspect and adapt their practices in order to improve performance. Additionally, the team members are the experts with the development work and are in a better position to determine how they can best work together compared to a project manager. After the practice has been implemented, it will be incumbent on the project manager to verify any performance improvements and bring that information to the project team during the next sprint retrospective so the team can further reflect and adjust. A burndown chart will capture the team's velocity and allow a before and after comparison to determine whether or not pair programming was successful in improving productivity. Any knowledge gained should then be captured in the lessons learned register. Note, the scenario implies the project is managed using hybrid methods. Therefore, incorporating pair programming may or may not require an approved change request. This would depend on how explicit the project management plan is in this case. Details for Each Option: A During the upcoming sprint, measure velocity and, if it decreases, discontinue pair programming and reprimand the team members Incorrect. Pair programming may need to be discontinued if the practice fails to improve productivity. However, there would be no reason to reprimand the team members. Doing so would likely discourage any future new suggestions from being surfaced. Teams need some latitude to experiment to find best practices that improve performance and should not face repercussions for doing so. B Invite the project sponsor and other key stakeholders to demonstrate the increased collaboration between the project team members Incorrect. While there is nothing wrong with the action described by this answer choice, it seems a bit too early and not necessarily helpful. The team wants to experiment. It's best to let them do this in a relatively familiar environment and make sure the practice indeed improves productivity before inviting the sponsor and other stakeholders to observe. C Submit a change request to update the communications management plan with the new agile practice of pair programming Incorrect. Pair programming is a software development technique in which two team members are sitting at one workstation and developing code in rotation while checking each other's work. The communications management plan describes how information is communicated on the project rather than how the software is developed or work performed. D At the end of the upcoming sprint, revise the burndown chart, evaluate the productivity, and update the lessons learned register Correct. The project manager should support the team with their efforts to improve performance. Once a new practice is implemented, the project manager should verify performance improvements to determine effectiveness.$res28$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $res28$RESOURCE-37$res28$;

-- Q38: RESOURCE-38 explanation (824 -> 2480 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $res28$Project leaders are responsible for creating a collaborative project team environment. Ideally, with an agile project, like the one described in the scenario, to take advantage of the benefits of face-to-face communication and osmotic learning, the project team would be physically colocated. However, physical colocation might not always be possible. In such situations, to support team collaboration, project leaders will need to explore virtual options, such as video and audio conferencing, messaging platforms, and other technological tools available nowadays. Although not ideal, under the circumstances described in the scenario, exploring virtual options for team collaboration until the key resource is out of quarantine and is able to join the team in person would be the project leader's best option. Details for Each Option: A Explore virtual options for team collaboration Correct. Having the team collaborate face-to-face would be ideal. However, face-to-face communication might not always be practical or even possible. Under the circumstances described in the scenario, to move forward with the project, incorporating virtual options to support team collaboration is the best course of action for the project leader. B Delay the project by two weeks until the key resource is out of quarantine Incorrect. Things not going as planned is a common situation for most, if not all, projects. Project leaders are expected to look for creative solutions to accomplish project goals under uncertainty. Simply delaying the project by two weeks until the key resource is out of quarantine is unlikely to be considered the best course of action for the project leader. C Acquire a replacement for the key resource Incorrect. Identifying and onboarding a replacement takes time. In the scenario, the key project team member came back from vacation just in time to start working on the sprint. Therefore, assuming that the replacement with the required skillset and project knowledge can be found quickly and begin delivering value would be highly unlikely. D Have the remaining team members swarm on the key resource's tasks Incorrect. Swarming is a method in which multiple team members focus collectively on completing a task. Having the team swarm on the key resource's tasks would address the original issue but create another one: tasks initially assigned to the other team members would be delayed, eventually having the same (or similar) negative effect on the sprint.$res28$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $res28$RESOURCE-38$res28$;

